{"title":"2024 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof_of_This_Claim\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof of This Claim</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(The_simplified_version_of_Solution_1)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (The simplified version of Solution 1)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_DDM\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by DDM</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_(First_fully_understand_the_problem!!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_(Goes_through_full_thought_process)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve_(crystal_clear!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve (crystal clear!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_NiuniuMaths_(Easy_to_understand!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by NiuniuMaths (Easy to understand!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_CosineMethod_[🔥Fast_and_Easy🔥]\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by CosineMethod [🔥Fast and Easy🔥]</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Fast_Solution_(2_minutes)_AND_generalized_formula_by_MegaMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fast Solution (2 minutes) AND generalized formula by MegaMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Dr._David\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Dr. David</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(1_min_visualization_technique)!\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (1 min visualization technique)!</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-20\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">19</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-21\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">20</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-22\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">21</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Rodrigo has a very large sheet of graph paper. First he draws a line segment connecting point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/9/559c5f1a789e7c0b85a47cea72d5217259ecb924.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> to point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/c/c0c67f1e6c098104b1cb8f1cdd6bb16f689ac3e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> and colors the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cells whose interiors intersect the segment, as shown below. Next Rodrigo draws a line segment connecting point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/7/2378fa15f4b3a7852ad20fe8e648e5e08b96735e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2000,3000)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" /> to point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee58d93372413161e65448d22c174036c9f6d4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5000,8000)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" />. How many cells will he color this time?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/a/54aaca68a9066392288fe300ed579d543dc2bccf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] // Asymptote code by aoum unitsize(8mm);  fill((1,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(1,2)--cycle, lightgray); fill((0,2)--(1,2)--(1,4)--(0,4)--cycle, lightgray);  for (int i = -1; i &lt; 6; ++i) {   draw((-1,i)--(5,i));   draw((i,-1)--(i,5)); }  draw((-1,0)--(5,0), Arrow); draw((0,-1)--(0,5), Arrow);  draw((2,0)--(0,4), black + 1.5bp);  dot((2,0), black + 6bp); dot((0,4), black + 6bp);  label(scale(0.7)*&quot;$(2,0)$&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(scale(0.7)*&quot;$(0,4)$&quot;, (0,4), NW); [/asy]\" width=\"228\" height=\"228\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/9/7a97030d74d56673c7672bfb251d5b4e1917706e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 6000\\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 6500\\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 7000\\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 7500\\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 8000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"492\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/6/9166ef883925e9f0447ca79c079786b35ad43671.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"18\" /> be the number of cells the line segment from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/b/c4b86f54134d69cad8793688a431aa907aaf2698.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/f/adfd9ae8a3fac031d3b8b470a52a709a23d4d6d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> passes through. The problem is then  equivalent to finding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/3/233aea4231046445b3386df8788ad35801395647.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(5000-2000, 8000-3000)=f(3000, 5000).\\]\" width=\"355\" height=\"18\" /> Sometimes the segment passes through lattice points in between the endpoints, which happens <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/a/6ea48ac847a997d226872960b91f84f763866094.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(3000, 5000)-1=999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"205\" height=\"20\" /> times. This partitions the segment into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee927e1332358c96c62c277441c907c4f51057f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> congruent pieces that each pass through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/7/4a76c774850cbaab3cb3eb3bcacaf5cddf2f7826.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(3, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"53\" height=\"20\" /> cells, which means the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a1d83d64d185b9c9d6c52569e18986cc39a7b09.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1000f(3, 5).\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> Note that a new square is entered when the lines pass through one of the lines in the coordinate grid, which for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/7/4a76c774850cbaab3cb3eb3bcacaf5cddf2f7826.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(3, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"53\" height=\"20\" /> happens <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/c/84ce3702e6f054e30cd07dcf23ff28347dbf18fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3-1+5-1=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"138\" height=\"15\" /> times. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are relatively prime, no lattice point except for the endpoints intersects the line segment from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/b/c4b86f54134d69cad8793688a431aa907aaf2698.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/8/b383072fa1f491732a33254bdd706582e58bbcb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3, 5).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> This means that including the first cell closest to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/0/ac02b61e084dddf32f5295ba51d90a70daad5e38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> The segment passes through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/a/d6ad23749cc12f549259b812cff1112e4d64f2b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(3, 5)=6+1=7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"151\" height=\"20\" /> cells. Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/1/b717820aeaa113b6f6930ca26512188b10c5ff04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}7000}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"83\" height=\"30\" /> Alternatively, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/7/4a76c774850cbaab3cb3eb3bcacaf5cddf2f7826.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(3, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"53\" height=\"20\" /> can be found by drawing an accurate diagram, leaving you with the same answer.\n</p><p>~BS2012\n</p><p>Note: A general form for finding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/6/9166ef883925e9f0447ca79c079786b35ad43671.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/6/83628e407071ccf625046edf5954a9f2d99f433f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y-\\text{gcd}(x, y).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" /> We subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/a/6da44de6ffa121233c9fb41e06cb90f67d3f0bfb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"69\" height=\"20\" /> to account for overlapping, when the line segment goes through a lattice point.\n</p><p>~mathkiddus\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_of_This_Claim\">Proof of This Claim</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof of This Claim\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/b/e7b3caffd84b992285eb557f60590e6c7f53bf4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Lemma 1 for Problem 23:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"217\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be relatively prime positive integers. When a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/5/345980cc755e001ed2c379e222a433ebd1071c6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\times q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> rectangle is split up into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/c/79ccffd0d1686be982eed9fdcb75f35551e8cb3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"11\" /> unit squares, exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/6/b26b2b039554ad802227ade8ea92ff1c1db8bff9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p + q - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"17\" /> unit squares are crossed by the diagonal of this rectangle. \n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/b/9bb898b0c22cf630955137452bc837b3942e4476.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Proof:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p><br />\nFirst, we claim that the diagonal does not cross the corner of a unit square. \\\\\\\\\nTo prove this claim we proceed by way of contradiction. Plot the rectangle on the Cartesian plane at the vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/4/1441cef6fbe9d2db31a28ce168f30c4b1d7e2356.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0),(p,0),(q,p),(0,q).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"192\" height=\"20\" /> The diagonal has endpoints at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/3/33331eb8c7bdf918ae84b9a8cbb42dd17038a0a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0),(q,p)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" />, so its slope is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/7/df7f739f24d0fac7aad7d69c88af0a6468e08e21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"20\" height=\"38\" />  Now, suppose the diagonal goes through the corner point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/8/8e8da0aef2d19017da7471378386d61620f288f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/0/4b0d67a9388fc84d745496e8efacddb6339b6945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/5/ab50fc028e50abe4a9b4b446cbcfc4ee2cffe5fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b&lt;p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />. The slope of this line is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/2/032cb0c5932179b6ceedd7baa2a7b33391437704.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{b}{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"12\" height=\"37\" />, which must be equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/3/8238e23ea62031f4c122beff87871a238dc39d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"17\" height=\"36\" /> implying that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/7347a1d5586e82c832f61ea799d89945ed2837fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> can be reduced, contradicting the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are relatively prime. We conclude that no corner points of a grid entry (unit square) are crossed. \\\\\\\\\nSince no corner points are crossed, each time the diagonal crosses either a horizontal or vertical grid line, exactly one more unit square is touched by the diagonal. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/0/ab0727c08c4aaa6fa119bd3fafe17851e431fd70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> horizontal lines and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/a/55af207869f43d97328748a5cb85274095a6a935.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"39\" height=\"15\" /> vertical lines, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/6/8165ea14d4957e778beef52d94308d3aa3f346a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p + q - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"17\" /> total lines crossed by the diagonal. This doesn't include the square in the bottom left corner, crossed initially. Therefore, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/8/c185efd679fba79f36a8e8baac9ad8deca259606.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p + q-2 + 1 = p + q - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"200\" height=\"17\" /> unit squares crossed by the diagonal and our claim is proven.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2745a5631b9003fd2a4504f87415a37ed5316c81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Lemma 2 for Problem 23:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"217\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><br />\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be positive integers. When a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/5/345980cc755e001ed2c379e222a433ebd1071c6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\times q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> rectangle is split up into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/c/79ccffd0d1686be982eed9fdcb75f35551e8cb3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"11\" /> units squares, exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/1/aa13cfead384b2fe52bd26e4d2db6f0fa0976e21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p + q - \\gcd(p, q)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"132\" height=\"20\" /> unit squares are crossed by the diagonal of this rectangle.\n</p><p><br />\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/0/fe076843256ac72cd493201b624a2136ea7034bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd (p,q) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" />, then we are done by Lemma 1.\n</p><p>Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/d/29d6964eb9cfc9e0c639014e5c014b7a1a4f986f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(p,q) = k&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"137\" height=\"20\" />, i.e <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c12994d64eb17e3de50fa790b3d8a4378cff94b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p = ak$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/a/eca10b1e258db52c5b91efbe8b36866bdfd95898.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q = bk$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, for positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />. We can then split the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/5/345980cc755e001ed2c379e222a433ebd1071c6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\times q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> rectangle up into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/b/06b4907901c2ec45cef89219dea97169895e364d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{k} \\times \\frac{q}{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"52\" height=\"34\" /> rectangles, strung together at the diagonal. An example for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/7/977c2ddb97dee9b2d09ac394d7865d7d2942bd03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(p,q)=(4,6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"107\" height=\"20\" /> is shown below, where two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/2/7b2acbb14d3d8c3be4519bb070578c630129e2bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> rectangles are strung together: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/0/0a048cb91bd449d2986a52a1df11085ec94a51e9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(1cm); draw((0,0)--(6,4),linewidth(1)); currentpen = linewidth(.5); for (real i = 0; i &lt;= 6; ++i) {     draw((i, 0)--(i, 4)); } for (real i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i) {     draw((0, i)--(6, i)); }  currentpen = linewidth(1.5); for (real i = 0; i &lt;= 3; ++i) {     draw((i, 0)--(i, 2)); } for (real i = 0; i &lt;= 2; ++i) {     draw((0, i)--(3, i)); }  for (real i = 3; i &lt;= 6; ++i) {     draw((i, 2)--(i, 4)); } for (real i = 3; i &lt;= 5; ++i) {     draw((3, i-1)--(6, i-1)); } [/asy]\" width=\"288\" height=\"192\" />\n</p><p>After the diagonal crosses the corner point of a square, the pattern repeats itself with the next one. By Lemma 1, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/d/e4d2004d9b91a2e87cbce24702fbcd3dd2456e3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{k}+ \\frac{q}{k} - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"83\" height=\"34\" /> diagonals crossed in each rectangle. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/3/d438fdbf60713b97f94e873c1699fa839eb39186.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = \\gcd (p, q)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" /> rectangles, so the number of crossed diagonals in total is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/4/ec45eac04215dac965c3ff6f6f84be5b5a162e9f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k\\left( \\frac{p}{k}+ \\frac{q}{k} - 1 \\right) = p + q - \\gcd(p,q).\\]\" width=\"273\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p>-Benedict T (countmath1)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28The_simplified_version_of_Solution_1.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(The_simplified_version_of_Solution_1)\">Solution 2 (The simplified version of Solution 1)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (The simplified version of Solution 1)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Draw a line in the lattice (rulers are allowed on the AMC 8) from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/6/876b66789c31005ef23e41081d8f7c10137faaa5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f2e89d3c8d6c890e9b5603d48822b828da99918.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5,8)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and notice that the line crossed 7 blocks in this pattern. Such a pattern is repeated 1000 times between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/7/2378fa15f4b3a7852ad20fe8e648e5e08b96735e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2000,3000)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee58d93372413161e65448d22c174036c9f6d4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5000,8000)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/8/d4809a02775445b0e438268ee788bf74749f8f88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}7000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"78\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>Note that since the grid only goes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/e/11e8da5448a5f378d5b8c253cdcd3cb49a90fa49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, a line could instead be drawn from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/3/f431d7e58fc836be21857261dc5b01d34f860471.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> so you don't have to manually expand the grid.\n</p><p>~Minor edits by mihikamishra\n~Minor edits by wr4th0fsh4dow\n~Minor edits by Michael0926\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ\">https://youtu.be/OwJvuq6F7sQ</a>\n</p><p>~Thesmartgreekmathdude\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_DDM\">Video Solution by DDM</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by DDM\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg\">https://youtu.be/29xcfUcmTgg</a>\n</p><p>~piggydude\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_.28First_fully_understand_the_problem.21.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Math-X_(First_fully_understand_the_problem!!!)\">Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=WqyvRC1PRp2FZIL9&amp;t=7181\">https://youtu.be/BaE00H2SHQM?si=WqyvRC1PRp2FZIL9&amp;t=7181</a>\n</p><p>~Math-X\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_.28Goes_through_full_thought_process.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Central_Valley_Math_Circle_(Goes_through_full_thought_process)\">Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Central Valley Math Circle (Goes through full thought process)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/_EGtDKsewuA\">https://youtu.be/_EGtDKsewuA</a>\n</p><p>~mr_mathman\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28A_Clever_Explanation_You.E2.80.99ll_Get_Instantly.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\">Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/5ZIFnqymdDQ?si=LEqWH6Q7azylK6do&amp;t=3409\">https://youtu.be/5ZIFnqymdDQ?si=LEqWH6Q7azylK6do&amp;t=3409</a>\n</p><p>~hsnacademy\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve_.28crystal_clear.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve_(crystal_clear!)\">Video Solution by Power Solve (crystal clear!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power Solve (crystal clear!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fzgWcEz4K_A\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fzgWcEz4K_A</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/wNymnFQfN_k\">https://youtu.be/wNymnFQfN_k</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\"